    {
      "id": "Xref",
      "code_hash": "b9e937fd9372989d0c3763376e900e62",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"2-the-rule-and-its-contract\">2. The rule and its contract</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">We'll write the rule in Ibis, state what it assumes and what it\npromises, and have the checker prove it before any data runs. The rule\nis plain Ibis; the decorator writes down what everyone involved is\ncounting on:</span>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>requires</code></strong>: what the job assumes about its input. Transaction ids\n  are unique, amounts are not negative, and timestamps are before 2100\n  (so adding five minutes can't overflow).</li>\n<li><strong><code>ensures</code></strong>: what the refund queue relies on. One row per pair,\n  the first transaction before its duplicate, a merchant on every row, a\n  non-negative amount, and the duplicate no earlier than the first.</li>\n</ul>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">The checks in <code>ensures</code> are the ones you would otherwise write as dbt\ntests (<code>unique</code>, <code>not_null</code>, <code>accepted_range</code>, an expression test) and\nrun on yesterday's data, hoping yesterday contained the bad case. Here\nthey are proved for <strong>every</strong> input that meets <code>requires</code>, before the\njob runs.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "SFPL",
      "code_hash": "aca0d037dbc70cde3c44b62eab8c0ee0",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": ""
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "BYtC",
      "code_hash": "c5cd3e3a0d54a5552b0c036ab3106b09",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\">Now we build it. The Delta table is a source, and calling the rule on\nit builds a node. Building the node proves the contract (a\n<code>VerificationError</code> would stop here), and its first refresh loads the\ncurrent snapshot:</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "RGSE",
      "code_hash": "a558b15b105644ad839ed12d26e03f43",
      "outputs": [
